<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  PLAYER play glyph.

  The canonical exporter records a vector's box, position and fills but never its
  geometry, so the box is what identifies the glyph - the same derivation the Mini
  Player's icons use. Here the box is:

    `Controls > play/pause > Container > Icon`   23.33 x 23.33, at (28.33, 28.33)

  `Container` is layoutSizing HUG, so it hugs its child and 23.33 x 23.33 is the
  ink rather than a canvas: the frozen glyph is exactly square, and centred at
  (40, 40) of the 80x80 control.

  #40 read that box as "a full icon canvas" and kept Material `play_arrow`
  ("M8 5v14l11-7z") on a 24 canvas rendered at 23. That paints 10.5 x 13.4dp of
  ink - 46% of the frozen box - and `play_arrow` is not centred in its own canvas,
  so it also landed 1.6dp right of centre. That is what made the control read as
  smaller than the frozen 80x80 even though the fill was the right size.

  So the glyph is written glyph-tight, as the Mini Player's are: the viewport IS
  the frozen box, the ink runs edge to edge, and the drawable's intrinsic size is
  the glyph itself. Centring it in the control then puts it exactly where the
  frozen design puts it, with no canvas padding to absorb.

  Untinted: the colour is `on_primary`, applied from the layout.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="@dimen/player_play_icon"
    android:height="@dimen/player_play_icon"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M0,0 L0,24 L24,12 Z" />
</vector>
